meta {
  name: Create asset
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/library/assets
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-School-Id: {{schoolCode}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "file_name": "ncert-maths-6.pdf",
    "mime_type": "application/pdf",
    "size_bytes": 482913,
    "sha256": "c3e8a0ff6971d17b440b40e15be0724c75c90ab7bf0fa9dee6eefdf904d95646"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 201
  res.body.status: eq pending
  res.body.storage_key: isDefined
  res.body.upload_url: isDefined
  res.body.upload_headers: isDefined
}

script:post-response {
  bru.setEnvVar("assetId", res.body.id);
  bru.setEnvVar("assetUploadUrl", res.body.upload_url);
}

docs {
  An upload is two steps and Bruno can only do the first one.

  This request records the asset and hands back a presigned PUT. The signature
  binds three headers -- content-length, content-type and
  x-amz-checksum-sha256 -- so the URL alone is not enough: a client that PUTs
  without repeating upload_headers exactly is refused by the store. Real S3 says
  SignatureDoesNotMatch; MinIO says 400 AccessDenied, "There were headers present
  in the request which were not signed" - different wording, same cause,
  not a helpful error from us. Note that the checksum in those headers is the
  digest base64 encoded, while the sha256 sent above is hex; sending the hex
  form in the header is the usual way to fail this.

  storage_key, status and the digest are the server's to choose. Supplying
  storage_key or status is refused with 422 rather than ignored, because an
  asset whose key the caller picked is an asset the caller can point at
  somebody else's bytes.

  The bytes themselves, with curl:

  curl -X PUT --upload-file ncert-maths-6.pdf \
    -H "content-type: application/pdf" \
    -H "content-length: 482913" \
    -H "x-amz-checksum-sha256: <upload_headers value>" \
    "<upload_url>"

  The link lasts 15 minutes. Nothing here uploads anything, so requests 5 and 6
  fail on purpose and say so.
}
